      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is CMC and why does it matter in cleaning product formulation?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "CMC stands for critical micelle concentration — the threshold concentration above which surfactant monomers in solution spontaneously assemble into micelles with hydrophobic cores capable of solubilising oils and greases. Below the CMC, surfactant molecules reside primarily at interfaces (reducing surface tension), but very little soil solubilisation occurs. Above the CMC, each additional surfactant added enters the micellar phase, increasing soil-solubilising capacity. In formulation, the CMC is the minimum effective threshold; real cleaning applications require concentrations well above it to maintain adequate micellar capacity under actual soil challenge. Formulations operating near the CMC with little headroom will fail when soil loading is high or when dilution in use brings the concentration below the critical threshold." }
